mpegv.library/--background--                   mpegv.library/--background--

    PURPOSE
        Decodes MPEG-1 and MPEG-2 video (a .mpg Program Stream, a VCD's
        .dat, a DVD's .vob, a bare .m1v or .m2v stream) by the CPU, for
        any program, as mpega.library decodes MPEG audio. A 68020 or
        better; no FPU needed.

        The decoder is libmpeg2 0.5.1, made faster for a 68k (its IDCT
        and motion compensation), its pictures libmpeg2's own bit for
        bit. MPEG-2 main profile 4:2:0, as on a DVD: interlaced and
        progressive, frame and field pictures, 3:2 pulldown flags.
        A VCD's picture (352 x 240) takes about 11.5 million 68020 cycles
        (vamos's count), a DVD's (720 x 576) about 79 million; under
        Amiberry's JIT that is about 4 ms and 26 ms. A real 68060 needs B
        pictures passed over (MpvControl's MPVC_SKIPB) for a VCD, and
        cannot keep up with a DVD.

    USE
        Open a decoder (MpvOpen) with a read hook that gives it the
        stream's bytes, and, for a Program Stream, a packet hook that is
        given its other packets (its sound: MPEG audio for mpega.library,
        private stream 1's AC-3 for ac3.library). Then MpvDecode, again and
        again: each picture in display order, the decoder's own Y, Cb and
        Cr planes (4:2:0, not copied), with its time in 90 kHz ticks. Show
        it with a helper: MpvToYUV422 for a graphics card's overlay
        (Picasso96's PIP), MpvToRGB to draw (WritePixelArray), MpvToGrey
        for an AGA screen of greys (WriteChunkyPixels or your own c2p).

        Without a read hook the program gives the bytes itself (MpvWrite)
        whenever MpvDecode answers MPVE_MORE, and says when there are no
        more (MpvControl MPVC_END).

        A decoder is used by one task at a time; different decoders may be
        used by different tasks at once. The helpers may be called by any
        task (they use only the picture they are given).

    HOOKS
        Called as the Amiga calls hooks: A0 the hook, A2 the decoder, A1
        the message; with the calling task's stack. In C (gcc):

        ULONG read(register struct Hook *h __asm("a0"),
                   register APTR mpv __asm("a2"),
                   register struct MpvRead *r __asm("a1"))

        The read hook may be called during MpvOpen (a Program Stream's
        first headers are read at once), and during MpvDecode, MpvPump and
        MpvGetInfo. The packet hook, during MpvDecode and MpvPump.

    TIMES
        All 90 kHz ticks, integers, as MPEG stores them. A picture's
        mpic_PTS is its own time stamp (its packet's, a Program Stream's),
        or carried on from the last picture that had one; (V2.2) a B
        picture shown before the first picture with a time (at the
        start, or after MPVC_FLUSH: an open GOP's first B pictures, as a
        DVD times only its I pictures) is timed back from the I or P
        picture after it by their temporal references. Set it beside
        its sound's (struct MpvPacket's mp_PTS). mpic_Time counts from the
        first picture by the fields shown (3003 ticks a picture at 29.97;
        a picture with its first field repeated, 3:2 pulldown, 4504 or
        4505), and mpic_Duration is how long the picture is shown.

    MEMORY
        Three pictures' planes, a 256 KB slice buffer and about 230 KB of
        stream buffers a decoder (a VCD: about 0.85 MB; a DVD: about
        2.35 MB), from exec (MEMF_ANY).

    LIBRARIES
        None but exec: libmpeg2 is integers throughout, and times are kept
        as stored (90 kHz), so there is no floating point anywhere: no
        maths libraries and no FPU.

    LICENCE
        The library is GPL-3.0-or-later (libmpeg2 is GPL-2.0-or-later);
        its source is at https://github.com/luvwagn/mpegv.library. This
        autodoc, the includes and mpegv_lib.sfd are MIT, so any program
        may use the library: one that opens it at run time is not bound
        by its licence. See LICENSE.

mpegv.library/MpvClose                               mpegv.library/MpvClose

    NAME
        MpvClose -- a decoder freed (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        MpvClose(mpv)
                 A0
        VOID MpvClose(APTR);

    FUNCTION
        Frees a decoder from MpvOpen and its pictures. NULL is ignored.

mpegv.library/MpvControl                           mpegv.library/MpvControl

    NAME
        MpvControl -- a setting changed, or a count read (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        result = MpvControl(mpv, cmd, value)
        D0                  A0   D0   D1
        LONG MpvControl(APTR, ULONG, ULONG);

    FUNCTION
        MPVC_SKIPB      value TRUE: B pictures passed over from here, not
                        decoded (nothing is predicted from them: a VCD's
                        are two in three); FALSE: decoded again. Their
                        times still count. Returns the last setting.
        MPVC_SKIPPEDB   returns how many B pictures were passed over.
        MPVC_ENDED      returns TRUE once the stream's end was reached.
        MPVC_FIRSTPTS   returns the first video time stamp (90 kHz), or
                        -1 if none has been read.
        MPVC_END        (without a read hook) no more bytes will come:
                        what is held is decoded, then MPVE_END.
        MPVC_HELD       returns the bytes held, read but not yet decoded.
        MPVC_FLUSH      (V2) after a seek: the bytes held and the
                        decoder's pictures are dropped; decoding starts
                        again at the next I picture (B pictures that
                        would need an earlier picture are passed over).
                        With a read hook, from its next bytes; without,
                        from what MpvWrite gives next. mpic_Number and
                        mpic_Time carry on; mpic_PTS is the stream's,
                        (V2.2) none carried on from before the flush.
        MPVC_HALF       (V2.6) value TRUE: pictures decoded at half size
                        (half as wide, half as high) from the next I
                        picture; FALSE: whole again, from the next I
                        picture. An open GOP's B pictures before that I
                        picture (which would need the one before it, of
                        the other size) are passed over. Returns the last
                        setting. Each picture's MPVPF_HALF says which it
                        is. For a CPU too slow for the whole picture: an
                        8x8 block's IDCT becomes a 4x4 one, the motion
                        compensation is made on half size pictures with
                        halved vectors; together a quarter of the pixels
                        and (measured on a 68020) under half the work.
                        The picture is about the whole one averaged 2x2:
                        an I picture to within rounding; P and B pictures
                        drift from it along a GOP (the half size cannot
                        hold the whole one's half pixel steps), most where
                        the picture moves in fine detail, and interlaced
                        pictures (field DCT and field prediction) are made
                        of half size fields, not the frame averaged.

mpegv.library/MpvDecode                             mpegv.library/MpvDecode

    NAME
        MpvDecode -- the next picture (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        pic = MpvDecode(mpv, error)
        D0              A0   A1
        struct MpvPicture *MpvDecode(APTR, LONG *);

    FUNCTION
        Decodes up to the next picture in display order, reading what it
        needs (the read hook, or what MpvWrite gave). The picture is the
        decoder's own: valid until the next MpvDecode, MPVC_FLUSH or
        MpvClose. An interlaced picture is both its fields, woven. From
        version 2, struct MpvPicture also has mpic_Duration (how long it
        is shown, 90 kHz), mpic_Fields (2, or 3 with its first field
        repeated) and flags: MPVPF_TOPFIRST, MPVPF_REPEAT,
        MPVPF_PROGRESSIVE (a progressive frame), MPVPF_FIELDS (coded as
        two field pictures). (V2.6) MPVPF_HALF: decoded at half size
        (MPVC_HALF): mpic_Width, mpic_Height and mpic_YRows are half the
        stream's (rounded up), and the picture is the top left quarter of
        its planes, their modulos the same (the rest is not written).
        MpvToRGB, MpvToRGBScaled, MpvToYUV422 and MpvToGrey take it as
        any picture.

    RESULT
        The picture, or NULL with *error (if error is not NULL):
        MPVE_END    the stream's end
        MPVE_MORE   no picture yet: call again (without a read hook,
                    after MpvWrite)
        MPVE_READ   the read hook said it could not read
        MPVE_STREAM not an MPEG stream mpegv decodes
        MPVE_FORMAT (V2) MPEG-2 that is not 4:2:0 (4:2:2, 4:4:4): the
                    library decodes only 4:2:0, a DVD's
        MPVE_NOMEM  no memory

    NOTES
        B pictures that need a picture the decoder has not had (an open
        GOP's first, at the stream's start or after MPVC_FLUSH), and
        every picture before the first I picture, are passed over, not
        shown; their numbers and times still count.

mpegv.library/MpvExamine                           mpegv.library/MpvExamine

    NAME
        MpvExamine -- the stream's size, pace and kind, all of it (V2)

    SYNOPSIS
        ok = MpvExamine(mpv, info, size)
        D0              A0   A1    D0
        LONG MpvExamine(APTR, struct MpvInfo *, ULONG);

    FUNCTION
        As MpvGetInfo, and the fields after mi_Kind too (as many of
        struct MpvInfo's as size bytes hold: pass sizeof(struct MpvInfo)):
        mi_Flags (MPVIF_MPEG2, MPVIF_PROGRESSIVE: a progressive sequence,
        else interlaced; MPVIF_LOWDELAY; MPVIF_PACKS2: MPEG-2 packs, a
        DVD's), MPEG-2's mi_Profile and mi_Level (4 and 8: Main profile
        at Main level, a DVD's), mi_Chroma (MPVCF_420), mi_AspectNum and
        mi_AspectDen (the picture's shape as shown: 4/3, 16/9) and
        mi_DisplayWidth and mi_DisplayHeight (the part meant to be shown:
        MPEG-2's display extension, else the picture's size).

    RESULT
        TRUE, or FALSE if no sequence header could be read (yet).

mpegv.library/MpvGetInfo                           mpegv.library/MpvGetInfo

    NAME
        MpvGetInfo -- the stream's size and pace (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        ok = MpvGetInfo(mpv, info)
        D0              A0   A1
        LONG MpvGetInfo(APTR, struct MpvInfo *);

    FUNCTION
        Fills info from the stream's sequence header (read first if it
        has not been): its size, its pictures a second (mi_RateNum /
        mi_RateDen, and mi_Period in 90 kHz ticks), its pixels' shape
        (mi_AspectCode, and mi_ShownWidth: its width for its height as it
        is meant to be seen), its bit rate, and what kind of stream it
        turned out to be. The fields mpegv 1 had (to mi_Kind): a program
        built for version 1 has a struct MpvInfo that size. MpvExamine
        fills the rest.

    RESULT
        TRUE, or FALSE if no sequence header could be read (yet).

mpegv.library/MpvOpen                                 mpegv.library/MpvOpen

    NAME
        MpvOpen -- a decoder (V1)
        MpvOpenTags -- the same, its tags as arguments

    SYNOPSIS
        mpv = MpvOpen(tags)
        D0            A0
        APTR MpvOpen(struct TagItem *);

        mpv = MpvOpenTags(tag1, ...)
        APTR MpvOpenTags(Tag, ...);

    FUNCTION
        A decoder for one stream.

    TAGS
        MPVA_ReadHook (struct Hook *) - the stream's bytes: the hook is
            given a struct MpvRead (mr_Buffer, mr_Length) and returns how
            many it put there, 0 at the end, -1 if it could not read.
            Without one, give the bytes with MpvWrite.
        MPVA_PacketHook (struct Hook *) - each packet of a Program Stream
            that is not its video: a struct MpvPacket (its stream id, its
            time if it has one, its bytes after its PES header: valid
            during the call). MPEG audio (0xC0-0xDF); private stream 1
            (0xBD: a DVD's AC-3, DTS, LPCM, subpictures), its first byte
            the substream, also in mp_SubStream (V2); private stream 2
            (0xBF: a DVD's navigation packets, PCI and DSI, whole); other
            video streams. Padding, system headers and stream maps are not
            passed on.
        MPVA_Stream (ULONG) - MPVS_PROGRAM, MPVS_VIDEO, or MPVS_AUTO (the
            default: a pack header or a sequence header first says).
        MPVA_ReadSize (ULONG) - bytes asked of the read hook at once
            (default 16384, at least 2048).
        MPVA_SkipB (BOOL) - B pictures passed over from the start.
        MPVA_VideoStream (ULONG) - (V2) which video stream of a Program
            Stream to decode: 0xE0-0xEF (default 0: the first found).
        MPVA_Half (BOOL) - (V2.6) decoded at half size from the first I
            picture (MPVC_HALF).

    RESULT
        The decoder, or NULL (no memory; or the read hook failed at once).

mpegv.library/MpvPump                                 mpegv.library/MpvPump

    NAME
        MpvPump -- one more packet demultiplexed (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        more = MpvPump(mpv)
        D0             A0
        LONG MpvPump(APTR);

    FUNCTION
        A Program Stream's next packet: video to the decoder (held until
        MpvDecode wants it), anything else to the packet hook. For a
        program that wants its sound ahead of the pictures (mpega.library
        reading from a queue the packet hook fills): when the queue is
        empty, MpvPump until it is not.

    RESULT
        TRUE, or FALSE at the stream's end (or, without a read hook, when
        it wants more bytes; or for a bare video stream).

mpegv.library/MpvToGrey                             mpegv.library/MpvToGrey

    NAME
        MpvToGrey -- a picture made 8-bit greys (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        MpvToGrey(pic, dst, modulo, levels)
                  A0   A1   D0      D1
        VOID MpvToGrey(struct MpvPicture *, UBYTE *, ULONG, ULONG);

    FUNCTION
        Writes mpic_Width x mpic_Height bytes at dst, modulo bytes a row:
        each pixel's brightness, full range 0-255 (levels 0 or 256), or
        0 to levels - 1 (a screen's grey pens, its palette set to match).
        For an AGA screen: WriteChunkyPixels (graphics.library 40), or a
        chunky-to-planar routine.

mpegv.library/MpvToRGB                               mpegv.library/MpvToRGB

    NAME
        MpvToRGB -- a picture made RGB (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        MpvToRGB(pic, dst, modulo, format)
                 A0   A1   D0      D1
        VOID MpvToRGB(struct MpvPicture *, UBYTE *, ULONG, ULONG);

    FUNCTION
        Writes the picture at dst, modulo bytes a row, in format:
        MPVR_RGB24 and MPVR_BGR24 (3 bytes a pixel), MPVR_ARGB32,
        MPVR_RGBA32 and MPVR_BGRA32 (4, alpha 0xFF), MPVR_RGB565 (big-
        endian), MPVR_RGB565PC (little-endian) and MPVR_RGB555 (2).
        (V2.3) Also MPVR_RGB555PC, MPVR_BGR565, MPVR_BGR565PC,
        MPVR_BGR555 and MPVR_BGR555PC: every 15- and 16-bit format of
        Picasso96's and cybergraphics'. A format it does not know: nothing
        written. BT.601, as pl_mpeg converts, 2 x 2 pixels at a time (one
        chroma sample). MPVR_RGB24 is WritePixelArray's RECTFMT_RGB,
        MPVR_ARGB32 its RECTFMT_ARGB.

    SEE ALSO
        MpvToRGBScaled

mpegv.library/MpvToRGBScaled                   mpegv.library/MpvToRGBScaled

    NAME
        MpvToRGBScaled -- a picture made RGB at another size (V2.3)

    SYNOPSIS
        MpvToRGBScaled(pic, dst, modulo, format, width, height)
                       A0   A1   D0      D1      D2     D3
        VOID MpvToRGBScaled(struct MpvPicture *, UBYTE *, ULONG, ULONG,
                            ULONG, ULONG);

    FUNCTION
        As MpvToRGB, the picture made width x height as it is written:
        larger or smaller, the nearest pixel (column x of the output
        shows the picture's column x * mpic_Width / width, row y its row
        y * mpic_Height / height), in one pass with no division a
        pixel: each source row it shows converted once into a row of
        memory of its own (AllocVec; without it, a pixel at a time),
        then copied out. Straight into a screen's bitmap in its own format
        (p96LockBitMap, or LockBitMapTags), with no RGB picture between
        and no ScalePixelArray. At the picture's own size (even), the
        same as MpvToRGB. Check the library's version (2, revision 3 or
        later) before calling it.

mpegv.library/MpvToYUV422                         mpegv.library/MpvToYUV422

    NAME
        MpvToYUV422 -- a picture made packed YUV 4:2:2 (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        MpvToYUV422(pic, dst, modulo, order)
                    A0   A1   D0      D1
        VOID MpvToYUV422(struct MpvPicture *, UBYTE *, ULONG, ULONG);

    FUNCTION
        Writes the picture at dst, modulo bytes a row, two pixels a
        longword (each chroma row twice): for a graphics card's overlay,
        which scales it (Picasso96's PIP: p96PIP_OpenTags, then
        p96LockBitMap its P96PIP_SourceBitMap and write here). A
        longword at a time: the card's memory across the bus.
        order: MPVY_Y0UY1V (Y0 Cb Y1 Cr: RGBFB_YUV422CGX as the ZZ9000
        shows it), MPVY_UY0VY1, MPVY_Y1UY0V (RGBFB_YUV422), MPVY_Y0VY1U.
        Which a card wants for which format is the card's driver's: try
        them on yours.

mpegv.library/MpvVersion                           mpegv.library/MpvVersion

    NAME
        MpvVersion -- the library's version, in words (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        text = MpvVersion()
        D0
        CONST_STRPTR MpvVersion(void);

mpegv.library/MpvWrite                               mpegv.library/MpvWrite

    NAME
        MpvWrite -- the stream's next bytes given (V1)

    SYNOPSIS
        taken = MpvWrite(mpv, buf, len)
        D0               A0   A1   D0
        LONG MpvWrite(APTR, CONST UBYTE *, LONG);

    FUNCTION
        Without a read hook: len more bytes of the stream (copied).
        Typically when MpvDecode answers MPVE_MORE.

    RESULT
        len, or 0 after MPVC_END.
